#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111763} :activity/id #uuid "cceda887-69b2-551b-ab27-e3d019c77f5a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049068 :topic/id #uuid "d78ca6bb-945f-51db-a4af-fc777024df22" :topic/math-academy-id 1459} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111900 :tutorial/content "We can organize all mentioned types of quadrilaterals into the following hierarchy diagram:\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nEach shape below another is a specific type of the one above it. For example, every square is a rectangle, and every rectangle is a parallelogram. This means every square is also a parallelogram." :tutorial/id #uuid "d9d3be2f-753b-5a4b-9f26-20b34ab8f89a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5931 :tutorial/title "Classifying Quadrilaterals"} :step/id #uuid "eab37537-55a4-56e5-9fd6-7ee823f295bb" :step/math-academy-id 36207 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111765}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111763 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111845 :tutorial/content "There are lots of different types of quadrilaterals. Let's look at some examples.\n\n- A **trapezoid** is a quadrilateral with exactly $1$ pair of opposite sides that are parallel to each other.\nThe parallel sides are called the **bases**. The other two sides are called the **legs**.\n\n![](images/9f/9fa09c46a2119d35c796065a696954931e20062fd5b3677a5484bef92bc2b0d8.png)\n\n- An **isosceles trapezoid** is a trapezoid with congruent legs.\n\n![](images/b6/b6b2b2012c1d724f9da8b23551ec4f978f190d86875d7db7a3074635d7f40c02.png)\n\n- A **parallelogram** is a quadrilateral with $2$ pairs of parallel sides.![](images/5d/5d0f383f3510f54b415a7ab99ef52fb30270924f97b8afec59534a96cd3edcef.png) Opposite sides and opposite angles of a parallelogram are always congruent. Moreover, if a quadrilateral has $2$ pairs of opposite congruent sides or $2$ pairs of opposite congruent angles, it is always a parallelogram.\n\n![](images/39/39ccf26333785f9ef47eed68a96bc40005a6ba025fdb6e250beefe14f10b043d.png)\n\n- A **rectangle** is a parallelogram where all angles are right.![](images/55/55a16e0ba93bf1f01d576cca558805aaea9631d1fa8e43b149509d4f8eb4c834.png) A rectangle where all sides are congruent is a **square**.\n\n![](images/a0/a04b2a0e86be8b085c5e3fc906d5aa66dfea45c033d52cd4746031202a7d427d.png)\n\n- A **rhombus** is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. The opposite angles of a rhombus are also congruent.\n\n![](images/d3/d3588d7cbe8f285198df3909df3c03fdec04f53a3f75769e46a0ab4283fa27b3.png)\n\n- A **kite** is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other.\n\n![](images/11/116e323d81ee190c0f69b4d7cdcefe9f0952da883b0d6d3c6d4036119794baa7.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "06a11fa2-5911-52f5-9a6d-1028e49b44dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2262 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "04c2712b-f67a-5317-b34e-8936b6acdd9c" :step/math-academy-id 9452 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111598}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111765 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110776 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110405 :question/id #uuid "515ab620-8513-55cc-8c88-61c0ad27aff2" :question/math-academy-id "e-2833"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00889b75-3745-52f6-9157-912846482689" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049584}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111261}] :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Quadrilateral"} :step/id #uuid "4f398499-a037-5d65-8f25-ecbcfa7534c2" :step/math-academy-id 9454 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111767}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111767 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110777 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110406 :question/id #uuid "ee3a028c-93df-5e5a-bc06-4c286a5e1839" :question/math-academy-id "e-2834"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c81cf5ed-8dd7-5174-b8e9-faf63bf0a278" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111264}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadrilateral"} :step/id #uuid "14f9869d-2b87-58dd-a3bd-3dfd6047425b" :step/math-academy-id 9459 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111768}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111768 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110778 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110408 :question/id #uuid "a7b82dde-36fc-582e-9ed6-fbfcfbf8b47c" :question/math-academy-id "e-2839"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34ed7bcd-3c06-592e-906c-5010e80370db" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111266}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Quadrilaterals"} :step/id #uuid "615b803e-ca7c-56d1-9167-9829ed84b5c5" :step/math-academy-id 9460}] :activity/title "Classifying Quadrilaterals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110831}]}
